From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Taixin Reverse <reverse@taixin.local>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:18:10 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] rnsh: enabled by default (allow-list still mandatory)

The shipped config said enabled=no, so the portal RNS Shell card sat
at off and S86rnsh skipped at boot. Enable it out of the box; the
service still refuses to serve until at least one allowed_id hash is
added (an unauthenticated root shell over public Reticulum is not
something this image exposes), and it announces its destination as
soon as it runs.
---
 .../board/cvitek/SG200X/overlay/etc/rnsbox/rnsh.conf      | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/buildroot/board/cvitek/SG200X/overlay/etc/rnsbox/rnsh.conf b/buildroot/board/cvitek/SG200X/overlay/etc/rnsbox/rnsh.conf
index 081586603..98618408d 100644
--- a/buildroot/board/cvitek/SG200X/overlay/etc/rnsbox/rnsh.conf
+++ b/buildroot/board/cvitek/SG200X/overlay/etc/rnsbox/rnsh.conf
@@ -1,7 +1,9 @@
 # RNS Shell (rnsh) — remote root shell over Reticulum. Portal-managed
-# (Reticulum tab). The service starts only with at least one allowed_id:
-# run `rnsh -p` on your client and add its hash here, one per line.
+# (Reticulum tab). Enabled by default; it starts serving once at least
+# one allowed_id is present: run `rnsh -p` on your client and add its
+# hash here, one per line. An unauthenticated root shell is never
+# exposed, so an empty allow-list keeps the listener down by design.
 
-enabled=no
+enabled=yes
 announce=600
 allowed_id=
-- 
